  story:[ "<b>The theatre is quiet now.</b> A monitor printout ends mid-case, while two nearly identical vials sit beneath the same fluorescent shelf.", "Nurse Beale, The Biomed Tech, and The Clerk can each show a different part of the center's routine, from the table to the service logs.", "The tempting stories are <b>A reckless anesthetist who overdosed the patient</b> and <b>A rare, fatal drug reaction — an act of God</b>. One supplies a villain immediately; the other turns the death into fate.", "The center reopens its operating list in eight days. After that, equipment will be serviced, stock moved, and the morning's habits folded back into routine." ],
  endings:{ overclaimWhat:"overdose", dismissalWhat:"reaction",
    win:{ expertTitle:"Physiology Restored to the Record", expert:["You identify <b>Lorne Halden — the surgical center's director</b>, <b>The Center's Administration Office</b>, and <b>A silenced alarm and a look-alike vial, normalized to save time</b>. Not a reckless anesthetist who overdosed the patient. Not a rare, fatal drug reaction — an act of god. The vial sequence, muted alarm, monitor trace, and standing order fit one clinical timeline.","Your finding explains why the patient deteriorated without a timely response and why blaming only the anesthetist would leave the normalized monitoring gap untouched."], soundTitle:"A Clinically Sound Finding", sound:["You place <b>Lorne Halden — the surgical center's director</b> at <b>The Center's Administration Office</b> and establish <b>A silenced alarm and a look-alike vial, normalized to save time</b>. The pharmacology and monitoring chain are persuasive, although the equipment chronology remains abbreviated.","The review board accepts the result because the chart, service record, and operating-room testimony reinforce rather than merely repeat one another."], namedTitle:"The Correct Clinical Accusation", named:["You name <b>Lorne Halden — the surgical center's director</b>, <b>The Center's Administration Office</b>, and <b>A silenced alarm and a look-alike vial, normalized to save time</b>, but provide only a thin account of how the vial and alarm defenses failed together.","The conclusion stands; a fuller anesthesia reconstruction is still needed before the center can redesign its medication and monitoring practices."] },
    overclaim:{ title:"A Villain Replaces the Physiology", body:["You choose <b>A reckless anesthetist who overdosed the patient</b>, turning a systems failure into one dramatic act. That claim cannot explain the standing alarm policy, the vial storage, or the delayed recognition recorded by several devices.","The exaggeration weakens the provable finding of <b>A silenced alarm and a look-alike vial, normalized to save time</b> by making careful clinical evidence sound like part of a personal prosecution."] },
    dismissal:{ title:"Bad Luck Silences the Monitor", body:["You accept <b>A rare, fatal drug reaction — an act of God</b>, although the medication layout and disabled alarm changed the probability of rescue long before the final collapse.","Calling the death unpredictable permits the same anesthesia setup to continue without restoring the very warnings designed to expose deterioration."] },
    wrongNames:{ title:"Right Failure, Wrong Custody", body:["You recognize <b>A silenced alarm and a look-alike vial, normalized to save time</b>, but assign the decisive authority or location incorrectly. The clinical chain still leads specifically to"] } },
